  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1238 PM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nAs of 12pm, the center of the ~994mb low is hovering right over the \nJames River Valley with its cold front extending southward into NE. \nEast of the James River is still within the warm sector in south to \nslight southeasterly flow where west of the James River is in \nnorthwest to northerly flow behind the fropa. Per marginally steep \npressure gradients and weak CAA on the backside of the low, winds \nwill start to pick up across central SD this afternoon with gusts of \n35 to 45 mph through this evening, with the highest gusts along and \nwest of the Mo River where a Wind Advisory has been issued.\nLocally higher gusts are possible. With this position of the \nlow/front, areas between the Mo and James River are within the \ndry slot with little to no rain falling as locations west of the\nMo is on the southern edge of the comma/wrap around with \nscattered light rain showers. Rain also continues over far \nNESD/MN.\n\nThe HRRR continues to show the threat for spotty thunderstorms \ndeveloping along the cold front (south of the warm front within the \nwarm sector) mainly across the Coteau and eastward this afternoon \ninto the early evening (~19-23Z). For this area, REFs SBCape is \npretty marginal (about 200j/kg) with a potential max of \n500-1000j/kg and wind shear of 25-30kts. However, mid level \nlapse rates are pretty weak with values of 4-5C/km. With this \ncyclonic flow around the center of the low, REFS actually \nindicates 0-1km SRH of 200- 300m2s2 and STP is up to 0.5 for a \nfew hours over eastern central SD (with the threat more south of\nthe CWA) and 0-1km shear around 20-25kts giving a slight curved\nhodograph. So to reiterate, severe weather is not anticipated, \nhowever, if convective parameters come together perfectly there \ncould be the potential for an isolated supercell or two capable \nof producing a tornado. Again the chances of this happening are \nvery low, but not zero. SPC does not have this region outlooked \nfor any severe weather with just general thunderstorms possible.\n\nBy 00Z, the center of the low will continue to slowly track \nnortheast and positioned over SD/ND/MN border with the low over\n~northeastern MN/WI by 12Z Tuesday. ENS is still a bit slower \nand more southwest with the track than GEFS at this point. This\nevening HRRR/REFS both indicate scattered rain showers ongoing \nacross western SD to portions of central SD (mainly north \ncentral SD). Models both show the rain really tapering off over \nmuch of the CWA by midnight, if not before, keeping coverage \npretty spotty (with the exception of far NE/SD closer to the \nlow). REFS/HRRR really does not have the rain filling back in \n(and really James Valley and eastward) until late morning or \nmidday on Tuesday with rain continuing over this area through \nTuesday night. A secondary low to our south (tracking northeast \nTuesday night into Wednesday across IA into MN/WI) will bring \nreinforced moisture, leading to more wrap around rain showers \nduring this time and even spreading back further west of the \nJames Valley Wednesday. The rain will finally taper off from the\nMo River eastward Wednesday evening into Thursday morning. \nProbability of additional rainfall amounts of one inch or more \nis 40 to 70% James Valley and eastward and highest along and\neast of I29.\n\nAs the weak CAA spreads eastward (and ongoing steep pressure \ngradients) the stronger winds will also spread eastward through the \novernight hours with gusts of 30 to 45 mph across the entire CWA by \nTuesday morning where it will remain gusty. Winds diminish a bit \nTuesday evening into Wednesday, but still remain breezy, with gusts \nof 25 to 35 mph. Overall, winds really do not diminishing until late \nThursday or so.",
